{"number": 165, "text": "                somebody/anything/nowhere,                              etc.\n       77\n\n\n                                                     ——\n                                                                                   ?   CHICAGO\n\n                  Somebody   (or Someone)       She has something in her      Tom  lives somewhere near\n                  broke the window.             mouth.                        Chicago.\n                   somebody/someone              something  = a thing,         somewhere   = in/to\n                   =a  person, but we don't      but we don’t know what        a place, but we don't\n                   know  who                                                    know where\n\n                People (-body or -one)\n                  somebody   or someone        ©.  There is somebody (or someone) at the door.\n                                               ©   Is there anybody (or anyone) at the door?\n                  anybody  or anyone\n                                               ©   There isn’t anybody (or anyone) at the door.\n                  nobody  or no one             ©. There is nobody (or no one) at the door.\n                        and -one are the same: somebody = someone, nobody  = no one, etc.\n\n                 Things (-thing)\n                  something      ©  Sarah said something but | didn’t understand what she said.\n                                 ©  Are you doing anything this weekend?\n                                 ©       angry, but | didn’t say anything.\n                  nothing        ©   “What did you say?” “Nothing”\n\n                 Places (-where)\n                  somewhere      (©) Megan's parents live somewhere in Southern California.\n                                 ©.  Did you go anywhere last weekend?\n                   anywhere      ©      staying here. I'm not going anywhere.\n                   nowhere       ©         like this town.